      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a very balanced perspective, and I see the value in looking at those 'shared metaphors.' \n\nHowever, I'm struggling to find the academic middle ground when discussing the *drivers* of these expressions. If we look at the works you mentioned—the Baroque painting or the Buddhist sculpture—they clearly involve a high degree of theological and philosophical abstraction that seems to drive the artist toward a pursuit of the 'ideal' or the 'concept.' \n\nIn contrast, when we look at many 'folk' or 'tribal' works, the focus often remains on the physical utility or the immediate ritualistic efficacy of the object. Some critics suggest this implies a difference in the cognitive stage of the society—that one is moving toward the 'transcendental' and 'abstract' while the other is more focused on 'immanent' and 'practical' realities. \n\nIf we want to maintain academic rigor in our symposium, is it possible to argue that Western art represents a specific evolutionary milestone in human cognition—namely, the decoupling of art from its utilitarian or social function—without it sounding like we're making a value judgment on the culture itself? Or is that distinction itself essentially a Western-centric construct that we're obligated to avoid?",
      "source": "evaluator"
