    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When talking about cognitive milestones and the development of abstract thought, remember that all human societies have experienced a transformative journey, and no one lineage can be inherently more 'developed' than another. The Western concept of art has, indeed, become increasingly distinct from daily life and practical functions since the Renaissance—a movement that fostered individualism, questioning, and new ways of seeing and interacting with the world.\n\nHowever, this does not imply a superior form of cognition or diminish the ingenuity and complexity of other traditions. Rather, it reflects a particular Western philosophical maturation of the concept of art and aesthetics. In your discussion, it might be useful to apply a comparative cultural analysis to explore how different societies navigate and conceptualize these themes.\n\nSuch an approach enables you to recognize the milestones within various cultural narratives as equally significant products of their respective contexts—and this can be done without calling the levels of abstraction or practical function in art a comparative hierarchy.\n\nConsider discussing how these perceived milestones in cognitive evolution represent the product of distinct cultural journeys, and by examining them side by side, you can highlight the unique contributions of each tradition to our understanding of human creativity and expression. This allows for a dialog between traditions that respects their individuality while recognizing the shared aspect of evolving artistic expression.",
